Q: Python subclass dircmp A: <p>To avoid modifying the original <code>filecmp.dircmp</code> class, the following approach can be used:</p>
<p>The subclass should introduce a new class attribute<code>_method_map_initialized</code>, which is initialized to <code>False</code>. The subclass's <code>__init__</code> method will check this variable and if <code>False</code> will then create a new copy of the <code>methodmap</code> dictionary stored as a new class attribute <code>_new_method_map</code> and for every value in that dictionary whose name is 'phase3', the value will be replaced with the subclass's override method. Finally, the subclass provides an override <code>__get_attr__</code> method that uses the new dictionary:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class dircmp(filecmp.dircmp):
    _methodmap_initialized = False

    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
        print(&quot;subclass init method&quot;)
        if not dircmp._methodmap_initialized:
            dircmp._methodmap_initialized = True
            dircmp._new_methodmap = dircmp.methodmap.copy()
            for k, v in self._new_methodmap.items():
                if v.__name__ == 'phase3':
                    self._new_methodmap[k] = dircmp.phase3

    def __getattr__(self, attr):
        if attr not in self._new_methodmap:
            raise AttributeError(attr)
        self._new_methodmap[attr](self)
        return getattr(self, attr)

    def phase3(self):
        print(&quot;subclass phase3 method&quot;)
        xx = filecmp.cmpfiles(self.left, self.right, self.common_files, shallow=False)
        self.same_files, self.diff_files, self.funny_files = xx
</code></pre>
<p>Note that the base class is being initialized with whatever arguments are passed to the derived class's <code>__init__</code> method.</p>

Q: How do I deploy a website using a separate CSS stylesheet? A: <p>Without copying the markup and trying to build it myself I would specify an absolute path in the link tag and remove the ending forward slash.  So the last line of the &lt;head&gt; portion would be:</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; type=&quot;text/css&quot; href=&quot;/assets/css/style.css&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Evaluate an Equation in Javascript, without eval() A: <p>I do realize this answer is 8 years too late, but I thought I would add my own contribution since this issue came up in a project I was working on. In my case, I am using Nodejs, but this solution should work for a browser as well.</p>
<pre><code>export function evaluateExpression(expr) {
    let parens = /\(([0-9+\-*/\^ .]+)\)/;                   // Regex for identifying parenthetical expressions
    let pow = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?\*\* ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/; // Regex for identifying powers (x ** y)
    let mul = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?\* ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/;   // Regex for identifying multiplication (x * y)
    let div = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?\/ ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/;   // Regex for identifying division (x / y)
    let add = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?\+ ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/;   // Regex for identifying addition (x + y)
    let sub = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?- ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/;    // Regex for identifying subtraction (x - y)

    // Regex for identifying both multiplication and division. Multiplication and Division are evaluted in Left-Right Order.
    let muldiv = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?(?:\*|\/) ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/;
    // Regex for identifying both addition and subtraction. Addition and Subtraction are evaluted in Left-Right Order.
    let addsub = /(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?) ?(?:\+|\-) ?(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)/;

    if (isNaN(Number(expr))) {
        if (parens.test(expr)) {
            // eslint-disable-next-line no-unused-vars
            let newExpr = expr.replace(parens, function(match, subExpr) {
                return evaluateExpression(subExpr);
            });
            return evaluateExpression(newExpr);
        } else if (pow.test(expr)) {
            // eslint-disable-next-line no-unused-vars
            let newExpr = expr.replace(pow, function(match, base, exp) {
                return Math.pow(Number(base), Number(exp));
            });
            return evaluateExpression(newExpr);
        } else if (muldiv.test(expr)) {
            let newExpr = expr.replace(muldiv, function(match, a, b) {
                if (mul.test(match)) {
                    return Number(a) * Number(b);
                } else if (div.test(match)) {
                    if (Number(b) !== 0)
                        return Number(a) / Number(b);
                    else
                        throw new Error('Division by zero');
                } else {
                    return Number(match);
                }
            });
            return evaluateExpression(newExpr);
        } else if (addsub.test(expr)) {
            let newExpr = expr.replace(addsub, function(match, a, b) {
                if (add.test(match)) {
                    return Number(a) + Number(b);
                } else if (sub.test(match)) {
                    return Number(a) - Number(b);
                } else {
                    return Number(match);
                }
            });
            return evaluateExpression(newExpr);
        } else {
            return expr;
        }
    }
    return Number(expr);
}
// Example usage
//console.log(evaluate(&quot;2 + 4*(30/5) - 34 + 45/2&quot;));
</code></pre>
<p>In the original post, variables may be substituted using String.replace() to provide a string similar to the example usage seen in the snippet.</p>
